<interrogationes unitas="unitates">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out what a file in the repository is (its unit, custody, lifecycle, role, language)?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_legere unitates_quid unitates_sutura_arboris UnitatisResponsum" imperium="bin/fabrica quid lib/xar.c">From
      the shell: bin/fabrica quid VIA (add -machina for TAB lines).
      From C: build a sutura with unitates_sutura_arboris(radix,
      piscina) (git ls-files -co plus disk reads), read the policy and
      declarations once with unitates_legere, then call unitates_quid
      per path. Each field of the UnitatisResponsum is a valor plus the
      causa (the rule that decided it), and responsum.unitas is the
      innermost unit, with parens links up to the root.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make my tool skip vendor, fixtures and generated files without a path list?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_iudicat unitates_viae_iudicatae" via="unitates.stml tools/iudex_unitatum.sh">Add
      an iudex line to unitates.stml (titulus, descriptio, and the
      curae, vitae, munera, provenientiae and linguae it accepts, each
      a space-separated list of known values), then ask it:
      unitates_iudicat(u, iudex, via, ...) per path,
      unitates_viae_iudicatae for a newline-separated list, or
      tools/iudex_unitatum.sh [-causae] JUDGE from a shell script. A file
      is judged only if ALL five properties are in the judge's lists; a
      file with no munus is never judged. Do not add an exclusion regex
      or path list: the judges replace the nine exclusion lists the
      spec counted.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I see why unitates_legere returned NIHIL?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_legere UnitatesRecusatio">NIHIL
      is a refusal, never a silent default. Pass causa_out to get every
      cause at once, one line each in via:linea: form, and
      recusationes_out (NIHIL is allowed) for the same causes as a Xar
      of UnitatesRecusatio. Typical causes: unknown attribute or
      element, a genus that is not citable, an unknown cura, vita or
      usus, the same title twice in one genus, a family with no
      library, no root unitas.stml, or unreadable STML.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free the sutura (or its piscina) after unitates_legere returns?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_legere unitates_quid unitates_sutura_arboris" via="lib/unitates.worklog.md">No.
      The Unitates keeps the UnitatesSutura pointer, because provenance
      is read lazily: unitates_quid, unitates_iudicat and
      unitates_interrogationes read files through it later. The sutura
      and its datum must outlive the Unitates; allocate them in the same
      piscina, as unitates_sutura_arboris does.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what unit does a file belong to, for example a test or a macos backend?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_quid unitates_legere" via="include/unitates.h unitates.stml">The
      innermost one, and a library beats the directory around it. A
      library stem S (include/S.h, lib/S.c or lib/S.m exists) claims
      those files, lib/S.*.md (worklog, phase-log), lib/S.*.stml (such
      as this file), docs/bibliothecae/S.html and anything under
      lib/S_assets/. A stem ending in a part (_macos _posix _interna
      _impl _tabula) belongs to its base library. probationes/probatio_X.c
      goes to the longest _-prefix of X that names a library or a
      directory unit (the library wins at equal length), else to the
      innermost directory unit (when that is the root, the census lists
      the test under orphani).
      Everything else goes to the innermost DIR/unitas.stml, and that
      declaration file itself belongs to the CONTAINING unit.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I mark a file as generated so the formatter and lint treat it that way?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_quid unitates_iudicat" via="include/unitates.h">Put
      GENERATUM in its FIRST line, or in the second line when the first
      must be a declaration (&lt;?xml or &lt;!doctype). The position is
      fixed on purpose: a hand-written header that mentions GENERATUM
      in its prose on line 2 stays manu. A judge that admits both
      provenances never reads the file at all.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I declare a new directory as a unit (an app, a vendored library, an archive)?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_legere" via="unitas.canon apps/vicus/unitas.stml vendor/unitas.stml">Create
      DIR/unitas.stml with a root unitas element carrying titulus,
      genus (a natura path that unitates.stml makes citable) and
      descriptio, plus optional cura, vita, successor and usus; foreign
      code adds origo children (via, fons, versio, licentia). vita
      vetusta and successor must come together. Any unknown value is a
      refusal naming the known ones.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does a file deep inside archivum count as archived when its own unit declares nothing?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_quid UnitatisResponsum" via="include/unitates.h archivum/unitas.stml">Inheritance
      through the parens chain: vita is the unit's own declaration, else
      the nearest ancestor that declares one, else viva, and
      archivum/unitas.stml declares vita archivata. cura works the same
      way, except that when no unit in the chain DECLARES a cura the
      default comes from the genus of the innermost unit (genus lines in
      unitates.stml), not from an ancestor's genus. So a unit inside
      archivum is archived unless it declares its own vita, and the
      causa field names the declaration (or genus) that decided.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what decides a file's munus (role), and why is it sometimes empty?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_quid" via="unitates.stml">The
      munus rules in unitates.stml, in order; the FIRST rule whose
      directory segment and extension both match wins. Extensions are
      suffix matches on the file name, so .interrogationes.stml works
      as an extension. If no rule matches, munus is empty with causa
      nulla regula munus congruit, and no judge will accept the file.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I list units with missing pieces, orphan tests or undeclared directories?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_census UnitatesCensus" imperium="bin/fabrica quid -census">unitates_census
      (or bin/fabrica quid -census) counts every enumerated file per
      unit and role without reading any file, then checks the policy's
      exspectatio lines per genus: desunt lists unmet expectations
      (archived units are skipped), orphani lists root tests matching
      no unit, indeclarata lists directories with code that fall to the
      root. It is a report, not a gate.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can unitates_interrogationes tell me that an answer's anchor symbol no longer exists?
    <responsum symbolum="unitates_interrogationes UnitatesInterrogationes" via="tools/interrogationes_fumus.sh">No:
      it only reads and checks the form. It never returns NIHIL, reports
      problems per file in recusationes (wrong root, unitas not the
      owner, missing or doubled quaestio or responsum, no anchor) and
      still returns an entry that has a question and an answer. Whether
      symbolum, via and imperium name real things is judged by the
      interrogationes gate, tools/interrogationes_fumus.sh.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are cura, vita, munus and lingua chorda values instead of enums?
    <responsum symbolum="UnitatisResponsum UnitatesProprietas" via="lib/unitates.worklog.md">They
      are open sets declared in unitates.stml, so adding a role or a
      language is a policy edit, not a C change; an unknown value is a
      refusal listing the known ones. provenientia is the one closed
      set (manu, generatum), because it is derived from the GENERATUM
      marker rather than declared.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
